 delivery</article-title><source>Anesthesiology</source><year>1985</year><volume>63</volume><fpage>545</fpage><lpage>547</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">4051216</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B11"><label>11</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kaufman</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Bondy</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Benjamin</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Peripartum cardiomyopathy and thromboembolism: anesthetic management and clinical course of an obese, diabetic patient</article-title><source>Can J Anaesth</source><year>2003</year><volume>50</volume><fpage>161</fpage><lpage>165</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12560308</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B12"><label>12</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Velickovic</surname><given-names>IA</given-names></name><name><surname>Leicht</surname><given-names>CH</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Peripartum cardiomyopathy and cesarean section: report of two cases and literature review</article-title><source>Arch Gynecol Obstet</source><year>2004</year><volume>270</volume><fpage>307</fpage><lpage>310</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14685890</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B13"><label>13</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Marhofer</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Greher</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Kapral</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ultrasound guidance in regional anaesthesia</article-title><source>Br J Anaesth</source><year>2005</year><volume>94</volume><fpage>7</fpage><lpage>17</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15277302</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Pre-operative chest PA shows mild pulmonary infiltration along the broncho-vascular bundle in both lung fields and Kerley B-line.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjae-59-353-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Intra-operative chest AP shows more aggravated peri-vascular infiltration and prominent Kerley B-line.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjae-59-353-g002"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>Chest PA at post-operative day 4 shows a lower amount of both pleural effusion and improved sub-segmental collapse in the left lower lung field than the previous one.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjae-59-353-g003"/></fig><fig id="F4" position="float"><label>Fig. 4</label><caption><p>Ultrasonographic images of the lower abdomen demonstrate the anatomy around ilioinguinaland iliohypogastric nerve. E.O: external oblique muscle, I.O: internal oblique muscle, T.A: transverse abdominis muscle, I.I: ilioinguinal nerve, I.H: iliohypogastric nerve.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjae-59-353-g004"/></fig><fig id="F5" position="float"><label>Fig. 5</label><caption><p>Nerve block needle can be seen near the ilioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerves. I.I: ilioinguinal nerve, I.H: iliohypogastric nerve, N.T: needle tip.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjae-59-353-g005"/></fig></floats-group></article>